  {
   "id": 90,
   "type": "MarkdownNote",
   "pos": [
    -3260,
    300
   ],
   "size": [
    620,
    1080
   ],
   "flags": {},
   "order": 4,
   "mode": 0,
   "inputs": [],
   "outputs": [],
   "title": "Note: how to run this",
   "properties": {
    "cnr_id": null,
    "ver": "0.30.0",
    "Node name for S&R": "MarkdownNote"
   },
   "widgets_values": [
    "## H3 Ref2VA shot-list pipeline\n\nOne shot per queue. Nothing chains, so shots render in any order and a bad\nshot is re-queued alone.\n\n### Run a sequence\n1. Point **Shot List Loader** and **Save Shot** at the same `project_root`.\n2. Set **Shot Index** `control_after_generate` to `increment`.\n3. Set batch count to the shot count shown in Shot Info. Queue once.\n\nRe-render one shot: set the index, `control_after_generate` to `fixed`,\nbump **take** on Save Shot, queue.\n\n### Resolution\n`1344x768` is H3's native canvas and both axes are multiples of 32.\n**1280x720 is not legal** - 720 is not divisible by 32. The loader raises\nrather than letting H3 fail. Crop to 1344x756 and upscale at conform for\ntrue 16:9 delivery.\n\n### Reference sockets\n- `ref_image_0..2` <- the shot's SUBJECTS: characters first, then props and\n  vehicles, in the order the script lists them\n- `ref_image_3` <- the LOCATION plate for this shot, always\nIn the prompt these are `<Picture 1>`..`<Picture 4>` - **1-based in the\nprompt, 0-based on the sockets**.\n\nCharacter sheets are a **horizontal 4-panel strip** (3/4 body, side, back,\nface). `ref_image_size: max` scales the SHORT side to 2048, so a horizontal\nstrip keeps every panel crisp - a square grid would not. Props and vehicles are\na single clean image on a flat background; they are never panel-cropped.\n\nThere is no style reference. Style is carried by every sheet and plate already\n- they are all drawn in it - and stated in the prompt text. Slot 4 goes to the\nbackground, which buys layout, palette, lighting direction and depth for THIS\nshot, and keeps one location looking like one place across every cut.\n\n`panel_mode` controls reference cost:\n- `auto` - full 4-panel strip for a single-character close-up, 3/4+face pair\n  otherwise. **Use this.**\n- `full` / `pair` / `face` - force it.\n\nReference cost scales with strip width. Three characters at full 4-panel is\n~50M pixels of reference encode per shot; the `pair` crop roughly halves it.\n\n### Audio references\n`ref_audio_0..2` carry one clip each. How many are used depends on the policy:\n- `clone` -> ONE TIMBRE SAMPLE PER SPEAKER. A two-hander feeds two. Feeding one\n  would voice the second character from the first one's sample.\n- `dub` / `dub_keep_foley` -> one clip only: the slice of the recorded mix,\n  which already contains every voice in the shot.\n- `generate` -> none.\nUnused sockets receive null. H3 takes at most 3 standalone audio references, so\na clone shot is capped at 3 speakers.\n\n### Audio policy (per shot, from shotlist.json)\n- `generate` - no reference fed. H3 invents ambience and foley, and it is\n  good at it. The mp4 gets H3's audio.\n- `dub` - your recorded line drives lip-sync. The mp4 is written **mute** and\n  H3's mix is kept beside it as `_h3.wav`. Lay the clean vocal in at conform.\n- `dub_keep_foley` - same, plus Demucs strips the vocal from H3's mix and\n  writes `_foley.wav` so you keep the synced foley under your own vocal.\n  Needs `pip install demucs`.\n- `clone` - a voice sample drives timbre and H3 speaks the line written in\n  the prompt's `<d>` tag. No recording session; expect some drift across a\n  long episode. Good for the proxy pass and for incidental characters.\n\n### Sampling\n4 steps / `euler` / `simple` / LoRA 1.0. SLA stays LAST in the model chain,\nwired to both the guider and the scheduler.\n\nIf identity slips, in order: raise `panel_mode` to `full`, then try\n`res_multistep` (roughly doubles time), then drop `sparsity_ratio` to 0.80.\n"
   ],
   "widgets_values_named": {
    "text": "## H3 Ref2VA shot-list pipeline\n\nOne shot per queue. Nothing chains, so shots render in any order and a bad\nshot is re-queued alone.\n\n### Run a sequence\n1. Point **Shot List Loader** and **Save Shot** at the same `project_root`.\n2. Set **Shot Index** `control_after_generate` to `increment`.\n3. Set batch count to the shot count shown in Shot Info. Queue once.\n\nRe-render one shot: set the index, `control_after_generate` to `fixed`,\nbump **take** on Save Shot, queue.\n\n### Resolution\n`1344x768` is H3's native canvas and both axes are multiples of 32.\n**1280x720 is not legal** - 720 is not divisible by 32. The loader raises\nrather than letting H3 fail. Crop to 1344x756 and upscale at conform for\ntrue 16:9 delivery.\n\n### Reference sockets\n- `ref_image_0..2` <- the shot's SUBJECTS: characters first, then props and\n  vehicles, in the order the script lists them\n- `ref_image_3` <- the LOCATION plate for this shot, always\nIn the prompt these are `<Picture 1>`..`<Picture 4>` - **1-based in the\nprompt, 0-based on the sockets**.\n\nCharacter sheets are a **horizontal 4-panel strip** (3/4 body, side, back,\nface). `ref_image_size: max` scales the SHORT side to 2048, so a horizontal\nstrip keeps every panel crisp - a square grid would not. Props and vehicles are\na single clean image on a flat background; they are never panel-cropped.\n\nThere is no style reference. Style is carried by every sheet and plate already\n- they are all drawn in it - and stated in the prompt text. Slot 4 goes to the\nbackground, which buys layout, palette, lighting direction and depth for THIS\nshot, and keeps one location looking like one place across every cut.\n\n`panel_mode` controls reference cost:\n- `auto` - full 4-panel strip for a single-character close-up, 3/4+face pair\n  otherwise. **Use this.**\n- `full` / `pair` / `face` - force it.\n\nReference cost scales with strip width. Three characters at full 4-panel is\n~50M pixels of reference encode per shot; the `pair` crop roughly halves it.\n\n### Audio references\n`ref_audio_0..2` carry one clip each. How many are used depends on the policy:\n- `clone` -> ONE TIMBRE SAMPLE PER SPEAKER. A two-hander feeds two. Feeding one\n  would voice the second character from the first one's sample.\n- `dub` / `dub_keep_foley` -> one clip only: the slice of the recorded mix,\n  which already contains every voice in the shot.\n- `generate` -> none.\nUnused sockets receive null. H3 takes at most 3 standalone audio references, so\na clone shot is capped at 3 speakers.\n\n### Audio policy (per shot, from shotlist.json)\n- `generate` - no reference fed. H3 invents ambience and foley, and it is\n  good at it. The mp4 gets H3's audio.\n- `dub` - your recorded line drives lip-sync. The mp4 is written **mute** and\n  H3's mix is kept beside it as `_h3.wav`. Lay the clean vocal in at conform.\n- `dub_keep_foley` - same, plus Demucs strips the vocal from H3's mix and\n  writes `_foley.wav` so you keep the synced foley under your own vocal.\n  Needs `pip install demucs`.\n- `clone` - a voice sample drives timbre and H3 speaks the line written in\n  the prompt's `<d>` tag. No recording session; expect some drift across a\n  long episode. Good for the proxy pass and for incidental characters.\n\n### Sampling\n4 steps / `euler` / `simple` / LoRA 1.0. SLA stays LAST in the model chain,\nwired to both the guider and the scheduler.\n\nIf identity slips, in order: raise `panel_mode` to `full`, then try\n`res_multistep` (roughly doubles time), then drop `sparsity_ratio` to 0.80.\n"
   },
   "color": "#432",
   "bgcolor": "#653"
  },
